<body>

    <div class="container">
        <header>
            <h1>Thermal Expansion</h1>
            <p class="subtitle">Interactive Physics Module</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Section: Introduction -->
        <section class="topic-card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-info card-icon"></i>
                <h2>Introduction</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>
                    When a metal rod is heated, its length increases. This phenomenon is known as <strong>thermal expansion</strong>. 
                    It occurs because particles in a substance vibrate faster and move further apart as temperature rises.
                </p>
                <p>
                    To visualize this, imagine a solid lattice of atoms connected by "springs." As heat energy is added, the atoms vibrate more vigorously, effectively increasing the average distance between them.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section: Microscopic View (Interactive) -->
        <section class="topic-card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-microscope card-icon"></i>
                <h2>Microscopic View</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Observe how the atoms vibrate more intensely as heat increases.</p>

                <div class="simulation-box" id="atomBox">
                    <!-- Atoms generated by JS -->
                </div>

                <div class="controls">
                    <label for="heatSlider">Heat (Temperature):</label>
                    <input type="range" id="heatSlider" min="1" max="10" value="1">
                    <span id="heatLabel">Low</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section: Formula -->
        <section class="topic-card formula-card" id="mainFormulaCard">
            <div class="card-header">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-square-root-variable card-icon"></i>
                <h2>Linear Expansion Formula</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>The change in length ($\Delta L$) is proportional to the original length ($L_0$) and the change in temperature ($\Delta T$).</p>

                <!-- Formula Display -->
                <div class="formula-display" id="formulaRender">
                    $$ \Delta L = \alpha L_0 \Delta T $$
                </div>

                <!-- Formula Actions -->
                <div class="formula-actions">
                    <button class="btn btn-outline" onclick="openExplanation()">
                        <i class="fa-solid fa-book-open"></i> Explain Formula
                    </button>
                    <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="openFormulaLab()">
                        <i class="fa-solid fa-flask"></i> Open Formula Lab
                    </button>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section: Coefficients Table -->
        <section class="topic-card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-table card-icon"></i>
                <h2>Material Properties</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Different materials expand at different rates. This rate is defined by the coefficient of linear expansion ($\alpha$).</p>

                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Material</th>
                                <th>Coefficient ($\alpha$) in $1/^{\circ}C$</th>
                                <th>Expansion Behavior</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Aluminum</td>
                                <td>$23 \times 10^{-6}$</td>
                                <td>High expansion (used in thermostats)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Copper</td>
                                <td>$17 \times 10^{-6}$</td>
                                <td>Moderate expansion</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Steel</td>
                                <td>$12 \times 10^{-6}$</td>
                                <td>Low expansion (used in construction)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Pyrex Glass</td>
                                <td>$3 \times 10^{-6}$</td>
                                <td>Very low expansion (heat resistant)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>

    <!-- Modal: Explain Formula -->
    <div class="modal-overlay" id="explainModal">
        <div class="modal">
            <div class="modal-header">
                <h3>Formula Breakdown</h3>
                <button class="modal-close" onclick="closeModals()">&times;</button>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body" style="padding: 0;">
                <p>The formula for linear thermal expansion is:</p>
                <div class="formula-display" style="padding: 1rem; font-size: 1.2rem;">
                    $$ \Delta L = \alpha L_0 \Delta T $$
                </div>
                <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"><strong>$\Delta L$</strong>: The change in length (how much it grew or shrank).</li>
